R.append({"quantity":"Q5d: velocity fields and gradients at N, S, Bridge (per-pixel Gaussian fits, HCN and HCO+ 3-2, native 0.25\")","target":"N, S, Bridge",
 "value":"N: clean rotation-like pattern, red to the east, blue to the west (butterfly sigma peak at the nucleus). Planar gradient (km/s per arcsec; HCN / HCO+): R<0.4\": 182+-22 / 191+-26 at PA 96/95 deg (PA of the direction of increasing velocity, E of N); R<0.6\": 133+-13 / 134+-13 at PA 85/85; R<1\": 87+-5 / 84+-5 at PA 78/77; R<2\": 57+-6 / 46+-2 at PA 66/69. The kinematic axis therefore swings from ~96 deg at 80 pc to ~77 deg at 190 pc to ~67 deg at 390 pc, as reported by Sakamoto+2014 (90 deg near the nucleus, 75 deg at 3\" diameter, ~60 deg at larger radii). S: east red (up to +100 km/s above systemic), west blue; gradient 138+-43 / 140+-28 (R<0.6\") and 138+-28 / 113+-17 (R<0.8\") at PA 108-124 deg (E-W, in line with the Sakamoto+2014 near-linear E-W edge-on feature), declining to 90+-35 / 69+-9 at R<1.4\" because of the mixed kinematics of the outflow base; the steepest gradient lies 0.5\" east of the nucleus (rotation curve bins in Q5e: v_rot,proj 7-19 km/s at R=0.2\", 58-62 km/s at 0.5\"), as noted by Sakamoto+2014 (largest gradient 0.5\" east of the S radio nucleus). Bridge: weak, 38+-8 / 36+-9 km/s/\" at PA 26-45 deg (N-S, along the arc) within R<0.6\". Velocity errors scale with a beam-correlation inflation (n_eff = n_pix/beam area).",
 "uncertainty":"see per-radius values (S errors 20-40%)","unit":"km/s per arcsec; deg","confidence":"medium",
 "evidence":"scripts/q5e_kin.py -> out/q5/q5e.log, q5e_grad.csv, q5e_pix.npz, q5e_kin.png","assumptions":common})
R.append({"quantity":"Q5e: line widths at native resolution (single beam at the emission peak) and in larger apertures, with beam-smearing correction","target":"N, S, Bridge",
 "value":"Single-Gaussian fits on the baseline-subtracted spectrum, ONE BEAM (peak pixel, Xb4b 0.25\"): sigma (FWHM) km/s, HCN / HCO+: N 70.4+-0.9 (165.8+-2.1) / 55.7+-0.8 (131.1+-1.9); S 49.6+-1.4 (116.9+-3.2) / 48.9+-0.7 (115.1+-1.5); Bridge 33.4+-1.2 (78.8+-2.9) / 38.3+-0.8 (90.2+-2.0). Beam-sized aperture r=0.12\": N 61.8/56.1, S 46.1/46.5, Bridge 29.4/35.4. Larger apertures (r=1\"): N 71/67, S 89/90 (FWHM 210/212), Bridge 29/33: the S width doubles because the aperture mixes the E-W rotation (+-100 km/s) and the outflow base; the N and Bridge widths do not grow. Peak spectra are not purely Gaussian (reduced chi2 0.5-8 depending on the aperture; N and S peak-pixel profiles have extended wings); the half-maximum widths agree with the Gaussian FWHM to 10%. Beam-smearing of the planar gradient (G x 0.24\"/2.355) is 13.5-14 km/s at N and S and 3.7 at the Bridge: intrinsic sigma (peak pixel) N 69/54, S 48/47, Bridge 33/38 km/s, i.e. a 1-4% correction in sigma. HCN is 10-25% broader than HCO+ at N (HCN/HCO+ peak-pixel FWHM 1.26, r=0.12\" 1.10), equal at S (1.02) and 10% narrower at the Bridge: no saturation broadening difference at S and the N difference is tied to the HCN-bright core. These are 3-8x larger than the cloud dispersions of Brunetti+2022 (CO 2-1, median 23 km/s at 90 pc) and within the nuclear range of Brunetti+2021 (10-200 km/s at 55-120 pc).",
 "uncertainty":"+-1-3 km/s (fit); +-15% for non-Gaussian profile","unit":"km/s","confidence":"high",
 "evidence":"scripts/q5e_kin.py -> out/q5/q5e.log, q5e_width.csv; scripts/q5g_summary.py -> q5g_virial.csv","assumptions":common})
R.append({"quantity":"Q5f: dynamical and virial masses of the N and S nuclear disks, Mdyn(N)/Mdyn(S), crossing times","target":"N, S, Bridge",
 "value":"Rotation (circular) enclosed mass M=R v_rot^2/(G sin^2 i) from the half velocity difference of the +-R bins along the kinematic axis (slit +-0.25\", intensity-weighted pixel-fit velocities). N (i=30 deg; PA 75 +-15 changes v_rot by <10%): v_rot,proj 46-50 (R=48 pc), 62 (97 pc), 67 (146 pc), 55-56 (194 pc), 61 (291 pc) km/s (HCN, HCO+ agree to 3%), i.e. a flat deprojected curve ~110-135 km/s: M(<97 pc)=3.4e8, M(<194 pc)=(5.4-5.7)e8, M(<291 pc)=1.0e9 Msun (i=40/20 deg: x0.6/x2.1). S (i=80 deg): v_rot,proj 58-62 (97 pc), 62 (146 pc), 51-54 (194 pc): M(<97 pc)=(0.8-0.9)e8, M(<194 pc)=(1.2-1.4)e8 Msun (insensitive to i in 70-90 deg, +-10%). The S curve is NOT a clean rotation law inside 0.3\" (v_rot,proj 7-19 km/s at 48 pc) and the S systemic velocity from symmetric bins is 2790-2810 km/s versus 2710-2740 at the nucleus peak (the S dense gas is dominated by the E-W disk plus outflow-base kinematics): treat Mdyn(S) as an order-of-magnitude (factor 2) value. Mdyn(N)/Mdyn(S) = 4.0 (R=97 pc), 4.2-4.7 (194 pc) nominal, 2.3-10 over the inclination ranges, compared with ~2.5+-1 at 1 kpc diameter from Sakamoto+2014 (CO 3-2 line widths, Sect 3.3). Virial masses M=1040 sigma^2 R (R=1.91 sigma_r, Rosolowsky & Leroy 2006; sigma = beam-smear-corrected peak-pixel sigma): N core (R=42-75 pc) (1.3-3.7)e8, (r<2\": R=113-148 pc) (4.3-7.3)e8 Msun; S (R=50-81 pc) (1.0-1.9)e8; Bridge (R=127-135 pc, sigma 33-38 km/s) (1.2-1.9)e8 Msun. Virial and rotation masses agree within a factor 1-1.5 at matched radii (N: 3.4e8 rot at 97 pc vs 3-4e8 virial at 75 pc; S: 0.8-0.9e8 vs 1.0-1.9e8), which is the expected result for gravitationally dominated (not outflow-dominated) dense gas in the nuclei. Surface density M_vir/(pi R^2): N core (1.6-2.3)e4, S (0.8-1.5)e4, Bridge (2-4)e3 Msun/pc^2 (dynamical, includes stars: upper limit on the gas), versus >1e3 for both nuclear disks in Sakamoto+2014 and 470 median for GMCs in Brunetti+2022. Crossing time R/sigma: N 0.75-1.2 Myr (core), S 1.0-1.8 Myr, Bridge 3.3-4.5 Myr; the outflow dynamical times of Q15 (1.2-2.2 Myr for 330-650 pc at 260-290 km/s) are comparable to the crossing time at S.",
 "uncertainty":"N M(<194 pc) 5.5e8 (3.3e8-1.2e9 for i 40-20 deg); S 1.3e8 (+-factor 2); ratio 4 (2.3-10)","unit":"Msun","confidence":"low",
 "evidence":"scripts/q5f_rot.py -> out/q5/q5f.log, q5f_rot.csv, q5f_rot.png; scripts/q5g_summary.py -> q5g_virial.csv","assumptions":common+inc+[A("virial","M=1040 sigma_los^2 R_pc with R=1.91 sigma_r; assumes virialised, density profile ~1/r; not corrected for stellar mass or outflow; dense-gas (HCN/HCO+) tracers sample the same potential only if the gas is rotating in it")]})
R.append({"quantity":"Q5g: SUMMARY - what the 0.24\" data say about sizes/kinematics for beam filling (feeds Q1, Q2, Q16) and which results are NOT supported","target":"N, S",
 "value":"(1) Compact dense-gas sizes (intrinsic geometric FWHM): N core 0.27-0.48\" (50-90 pc; HCO+ 0.27, HCN 0.48), S 0.39-0.52\" (75-100 pc; HCO+ 0.32-0.39, HCN 0.48-0.52), i.e. 1.1-2x the beam; the 1.1 mm continuum at S is 0.21-0.26\" (40-50 pc, marginal). Beam dilution at 0.25\": for Gaussian cores of 0.3-0.5\" the peak is 60-80% of the intrinsic one (theta_s^2/(theta_s^2+theta_b^2)); at the 0.93\" Xb4d beam 12-27% (Q8g filling 0.14-0.18 agrees). (2) Both nuclei show resolved rotation with the kinematic axes of Sakamoto+2014 and central sigma peaks (N 55-70, S 47-50 km/s at one beam), and enclosed masses (0.3-0.6)e9 (N, <100-200 pc) and ~1e8 (S): the dense gas is gravitationally bound in the nuclear disks (virial ~ rotation), so line widths there are NOT evidence for outflow broadening; the broad S wings (Q3, Q13) and the 90 km/s aperture sigma at r=1\" are the outflow base. (3) Line-continuum offsets are consistent with zero (<=0.1\" at S, N core). (4) Not supported: '>=5 N sub-clumps' (3-4 matched), a single-Gaussian size for N (size depends on fit radius by x1.5-2.5), a continuum size of S below 40 pc. (5) For the equal-filling assumption of RADEX: HCN is 1.2-1.6x larger than HCO+ at S and 1.8x at the N core in the compact component (HCN 0.48\" vs HCO+ 0.27\" geometric FWHM at r<0.6\"), so a common filling factor is not supported by the sizes (N sizes depend on the fit model, see Q5b). Needed to go further: CO 3-2/2-1 and HCN 1-0 at <=0.3\" (not in the archive) for sigma(R) and inclination-independent masses; all sizes are of the compact (Xb4b-recovered) component.",
 "uncertainty":"see Q5b-f","unit":None,"confidence":"medium",
 "evidence":"Q5a-f; out/q5/ logs; scripts/q5*.py","assumptions":common})
